libc++abi: create the thread_local destructor key when libc++abi is loaded

At thread exit the pthread key destructors run in the order in which the
keys were created: Darwin hands out the lowest free key and walks the keys
upwards, PTHREAD_DESTRUCTOR_ITERATIONS times (Libc-594 on 10.6 and current
libpthread alike). GCC implements thread_local with emulated TLS, and
libgcc's emutls frees all TLS storage of a thread from the destructor of a
key of its own, which it creates at the first TLS access in the process.
The key of __cxa_thread_atexit() was created at its first call, which is
always later: a thread_local is accessed, and constructed in its emutls
storage, before its destructor is registered. So at thread exit the emutls
storage was released first and the destructors then ran on freed memory.
Darwin's malloc writes its free-list pointers into freed blocks, so the
first members of the objects are already overwritten when their
destructors read them.

Patch 0011 moved the destructor list out of TLS, but the objects still live
there. Create the key from a constructor function when libc++abi is loaded
instead. libc++abi depends only on libSystem, so its initializers run
before those of any code using it, and thus before that code's first TLS
access; the key's destructor then runs first, while the objects exist. If
emulated TLS was used before libc++abi was loaded (dlopen() into a process
which already used it), the order is the same as before.

DtorsManager, whose destructor runs the destructors of the main thread at
exit, stays a function-local static: constructed at load time, its
destructor would run after those of all other objects with static storage
duration. Only the key creation moves; a once flag serves both paths.

Checked on Linux with libgcc's __emutls_get_address and this file built
with -D__APPLE__: without the change the destructor of a thread_local reads
freed memory (glibc.malloc.perturb), with it the object is intact.

diff --git a/libcxxabi/src/cxa_thread_atexit.cpp b/libcxxabi/src/cxa_thread_atexit.cpp
--- a/libcxxabi/src/cxa_thread_atexit.cpp
+++ b/libcxxabi/src/cxa_thread_atexit.cpp
@@ -75,6 +75,7 @@
   // key destructor of its own, which is created earlier and therefore runs
   // earlier, after which thread_locals read as freshly zero-initialized.
   std::__libcpp_tls_key dtors_key;
+  std::__libcpp_exec_once_flag dtors_key_once = _LIBCPP_EXEC_ONCE_INITIALIZER;
 
   void run_dtors(void* p) {
     // The key's destructor is given the list after the key was reset; put it
@@ -90,14 +91,28 @@
     }
   }
 
+  void create_dtors_key() {
+    // There is intentionally no matching std::__libcpp_tls_delete call, as
+    // __cxa_thread_atexit() may be called arbitrarily late (for example, from
+    // global destructors or atexit() handlers).
+    if (std::__libcpp_tls_create(&dtors_key, run_dtors) != 0) {
+      __abort_message("std::__libcpp_tls_create() failed in __cxa_thread_atexit()");
+    }
+  }
+
+#if defined(__APPLE__)
+  // Key destructors run in creation order, and emutls frees a thread's TLS
+  // storage from the destructor of a key it creates at the first TLS access.
+  // Create this key before that, when libc++abi is loaded, so that the
+  // thread_local destructors run while their objects still exist.
+  __attribute__((constructor)) void create_dtors_key_at_load() {
+    std::__libcpp_execute_once(&dtors_key_once, create_dtors_key);
+  }
+#endif
+
   struct DtorsManager {
     DtorsManager() {
-      // There is intentionally no matching std::__libcpp_tls_delete call, as
-      // __cxa_thread_atexit() may be called arbitrarily late (for example, from
-      // global destructors or atexit() handlers).
-      if (std::__libcpp_tls_create(&dtors_key, run_dtors) != 0) {
-        __abort_message("std::__libcpp_tls_create() failed in __cxa_thread_atexit()");
-      }
+      std::__libcpp_execute_once(&dtors_key_once, create_dtors_key);
     }
 
     ~DtorsManager() {
